Synthetic Data Design Plan (User and Product Tables)

Product Table Columns 
| Column          | Type          | Why it matters                        |
| --------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| `product_id`    | int           | Unique ID for tracking and joins      |
| `title`         | text          | For keyword retrieval (TF-IDF / BM25) |
| `brand`         | categorical   | Trust / quality signal                |
| `category`      | categorical   | High-level grouping (e.g. “Shoes”)    |
| `sub_category`  | categorical   | More specific (e.g. “Heels”)          |
| `price`         | numeric       | Used by budget shoppers               |
| `rating`        | numeric (0–5) | Used by quality shoppers              |
| `num_purchases` | numeric       | Proxy for popularity                  |
| `color`         | categorical   | Optional filter or diversity feature  |
| `size`          | categorical   | Optional filter                       |
| `delivery_days` | numeric       | Useful if we want to simulate urgency |

Number of Records in Product Table : 200 - 500

- Numeric features : price, rating, num_purchases, delivery_days
- Categorical Features : brand , category , sub-category, color , size 

Making the Synthetic Data Look more Realistic 
Price :- use a skewed distribution (most cheap, few expensive). For example, use np.random.lognormal(mean, sigma) instead of random uniform.

Ratings :- mostly 3–5 stars. very few low ratings. Use random choice weighted like [1:0.05, 2:0.1, 3:0.25, 4:0.35, 5:0.25].

Num_purchases :- heavy-tailed (long tail): a few popular products get huge counts, most get low counts. Again, lognormal fits well.

Delivery_days :- small range, say 1–7.

Category/Subcategory :- pick from a list, but some combinations more common (e.g., “Sneakers” more than “Boots”)

User Table Fields :
| Column                  | Purpose                                            |
| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| `user_id`               | unique ID                                          |
| `persona_type`          | Budget or Quality                                  |
| `preferred_category`    | optional — helps simulate realistic clicks         |
| `avg_budget`            | optional — budget shoppers will have lower average |
| `avg_rating_preference` | optional — quality shoppers prefer 4+              |

Number of Records in User table : Total 100 , 50 Budget Persona and 50 Quality Persona initially later 